Company name clusters
=====================

company_clusters.json holds clusters of names of the same legal entity. It was
built on the date in its "fetched" field by build_company_names_fixture.py from
two public sources. The companies are picked by hand: the CIK and LEI lists are
in that script. The file has no other data from the source records.

SEC EDGAR
---------

Names and former names come from the submissions records at
https://data.sec.gov/submissions/, and the hard negatives come from the ticker
list at https://www.sec.gov/files/company_tickers.json. Terms were read on
2026-09-26.

From https://www.sec.gov/about/privacy-information:

  "Information presented on sec.gov is considered public information and may be
  copied or further distributed by users of the web site without the SEC’s
  permission. Please consider appropriate citation to the SEC as the source."

Source: U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, EDGAR.

The builder script declares its contact in the User-Agent header and stays under
the request limit that SEC states at
https://www.sec.gov/search-filings/edgar-search-assistance/accessing-edgar-data
("Current max request rate: 10 requests/second.").

GLEIF
-----

Legal names and other names come from the Global LEI Index records at
https://api.gleif.org/api/v1/lei-records. GLEIF publishes this data under the
CC0 1.0 Universal public domain dedication:
https://www.gleif.org/en/about/open-data

Selection
---------

- SEC: 34 registrants. A cluster is the current name plus every former name,
  with names that differ only by case or surrounding space merged. Registrants
  with no former name give single-name clusters.
- SEC hard negatives: for each registrant, the most similar name (token sort
  ratio of at least 0.8) of another registrant in the ticker list, as its own
  cluster.
- GLEIF: 21 records. A cluster is the legal name plus every other name of the
  record. GLEIF lists other names of the same legal entity, so each cluster is
  one entity by definition. Records with no other name give single-name
  clusters.
- The two sources are not joined. No identifier links a SEC registrant to a
  GLEIF record, and a join by name would assume what the test measures.
